    <article
        style="max-width:720px;margin:0 auto;padding:2rem 1rem;font-family:'Noto Sans JP','Hiragino Kaku Gothic ProN',sans-serif;line-height:1.9;color:#e0e0e0;background:#0a0a0a"
    >
        <h1 style="font-size:1.6rem;margin-bottom:0.5rem;color:#fff">
            Yamato Layout — The Best Keyboard Layout for Japanese Input
        </h1>
        <p style="color:#888;margin-bottom:2rem">
            大和配列 — 日本語入力に最適化されたキーボード配列 |
            <a href="/yamato/about" style="color:#e8c47c">日本語版</a>
        </p>

        <nav
            style="background:#111;border:1px solid #222;border-radius:8px;padding:1rem;margin-bottom:2rem"
        >
            <strong style="color:#ccc">Contents</strong>
            <ol style="margin:0.5rem 0 0 1.2rem;color:#888">
                <li><a href="#overview" style="color:#e8c47c">Overview</a></li>
                <li>
                    <a href="#problem" style="color:#e8c47c"
                        >The Problem with QWERTY</a
                    >
                </li>
                <li>
                    <a href="#layout" style="color:#e8c47c">Key Layout</a>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <a href="#features" style="color:#e8c47c">Key Features</a>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <a href="#comparison" style="color:#e8c47c"
                        >Comparison with Other Layouts</a
                    >
                </li>
                <li>
                    <a href="#install" style="color:#e8c47c">Installation</a>
                </li>
                <li><a href="#faq" style="color:#e8c47c">FAQ</a></li>
            </ol>
        </nav>

        <section id="overview">
            <h2
                style="font-size:1.2rem;color:#fff;border-bottom:1px solid #333;padding-bottom:0.5rem"
            >
                Overview
            </h2>
            <p>
                Yamato Layout (大和配列, "yamato hairetsu") is a keyboard layout
                designed for efficient Japanese romaji input. While QWERTY was
                created in the 1870s to prevent typewriter jams, Yamato Layout
                was built from the ground up to minimize finger movement and
                maximize typing efficiency for Japanese.
            </p>
            <p>Key advantages:</p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <strong>Easy to learn</strong> — Consonants follow the Japanese
                    syllabary order (50-on)
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Reduces fatigue</strong> — High-frequency keys concentrated
                    on the home row
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>No special hardware</strong> — Works on any standard QWERTY
                    keyboard via software. No custom keycaps or hardware modifications
                    needed
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Multilingual</strong> — Also optimized for English, Chinese
                    (Pinyin), and Korean input
                </li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section id="problem">
            <h2
                style="font-size:1.2rem;color:#fff;border-bottom:1px solid #333;padding-bottom:0.5rem"
            >
                The Problem with QWERTY for Japanese
            </h2>
            <p>
                Japanese romaji input follows a "consonant + vowel" pattern for
                every syllable (e.g., "ka" = K + A, "shi" = S + I). This means
                vowels appear in virtually every syllable. However, QWERTY
                scatters the vowels (A, I, U, E, O) across the keyboard:
            </p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <strong>Vowels are scattered</strong>: A (home row far
                    left), I (top row right), U (top row left), E (top row far
                    left), O (top row right) — fingers constantly leave the home
                    position
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Wasted home keys</strong>: QWERTY's home keys F and
                    J are among the least-used characters in Japanese
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Excessive finger travel</strong>: Constant reaching
                    from home position causes fatigue over long typing sessions
                </li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section id="layout">
            <h2
                style="font-size:1.2rem;color:#fff;border-bottom:1px solid #333;padding-bottom:0.5rem"
            >
                Key Layout
            </h2>
            <pre
                style="background:#111;border:1px solid #222;border-radius:8px;padding:1rem;overflow-x:auto;font-family:'JetBrains Mono',monospace;font-size:0.85rem;color:#ccc">
┌─────────────────────────┬─────────────────────────────┐
│  Left hand (Consonants) │  Right hand (Vowels+Other)  │
├─────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────┤
│  M    Y    R    W    P  │  X    L    Q    C    '  [  ] │
│  K    S    T   [N]   H  │  F   [A]   O    I    E   /  │
│  Z    D    B    G    J  │  V    U    -    ,    .       │
└─────────────────────────┴─────────────────────────────┘
[N] [A] = Home position (index fingers)

Left side = Consonant keys    Right side = Vowel keys + low-frequency
            </pre>
            <p>
                Yamato Layout uses your existing QWERTY physical keyboard. The
                physical key positions are the same — only the character
                assignments change. On Windows, AutoHotkey remaps the input,
                so no keyboard replacement, special hardware, or custom keycaps
                are needed.
            </p>
        </section>

        <section id="features">
            <h2
                style="font-size:1.2rem;color:#fff;border-bottom:1px solid #333;padding-bottom:0.5rem"
            >
                Key Features
            </h2>

            <h3 style="color:#e8c47c;font-size:1rem">
                1. Consonants Left, Vowels Right
            </h3>
            <p>
                Japanese romaji input is a repetition of "consonant → vowel"
                (e.g., "ka" = K → A). Yamato places all consonants on the left
                hand and all vowels on the right, creating a natural left-right
                alternating rhythm. This separation is also effective for
                English typing.
            </p>

            <h3 style="color:#e8c47c;font-size:1rem">
                2. Consonants in Japanese Syllabary Order
            </h3>
            <p>
                Consonant keys follow the Japanese 50-on syllabary order: K(ka),
                S(sa), T(ta), N(na), H(ha) on the home row, and M(ma), Y(ya),
                R(ra), W(wa) on the top row. You don't need to "memorize" the
                layout — you can "recall" it from what you already know. The
                name "Yamato" (大和, the ancient name for Japan) comes from
                this: the sounds of the Japanese language (K-S-T-N-H-M-Y-R-W)
                are literally arranged on the keyboard.
            </p>

            <h3 style="color:#e8c47c;font-size:1rem">
                3. Voiced Consonants Separated to Bottom Row
            </h3>
            <p>
                All voiced consonants (ga, za, da, ba rows) are on the bottom
                row: Z, D, B, G, J. Mnemonic: "Zunda Burger" (ずんだバーガー) =
                Z, D, B, G, J.
            </p>

            <h3 style="color:#e8c47c;font-size:1rem">
                4. High-Frequency Keys on Home Position
            </h3>
            <p>
                Based on Japanese text frequency analysis, the most-used
                characters are placed on the home row:
            </p>
            <table
                style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;margin:1rem 0;font-size:0.9rem"
            >
                <thead>
                    <tr style="border-bottom:2px solid #333">
                        <th style="text-align:left;padding:0.5rem;color:#888"
                            >Frequency</th
                        >
                        <th style="text-align:left;padding:0.5rem;color:#888"
                            >Keys</th
                        >
                        <th style="text-align:left;padding:0.5rem;color:#888"
                            >Yamato Position</th
                        >
                        <th style="text-align:left;padding:0.5rem;color:#888"
                            >QWERTY Position</th
                        >
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid #222"
                        ><td style="padding:0.4rem">★★★★</td><td
                            style="padding:0.4rem">A, K, O</td
                        ><td style="padding:0.4rem;color:#4a9">Home row</td><td
                            style="padding:0.4rem;color:#c66">Scattered</td
                        ></tr
                    >
                    <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid #222"
                        ><td style="padding:0.4rem">★★★</td><td
                            style="padding:0.4rem">I, N, U, T</td
                        ><td style="padding:0.4rem;color:#4a9">Home row</td><td
                            style="padding:0.4rem;color:#c66">Scattered</td
                        ></tr
                    >
                    <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid #222"
                        ><td style="padding:0.4rem">★★</td><td
                            style="padding:0.4rem">E, S, R</td
                        ><td style="padding:0.4rem;color:#4a9"
                            >Home row / Top</td
                        ><td style="padding:0.4rem;color:#c66">Scattered</td
                        ></tr
                    >
                    <tr
                        ><td style="padding:0.4rem">★</td><td
                            style="padding:0.4rem">Others</td
                        ><td style="padding:0.4rem">Top / Bottom</td><td
                            style="padding:0.4rem">Scattered</td
                        ></tr
                    >
                </tbody>
            </table>

            <h3 style="color:#e8c47c;font-size:1rem">
                5. Multilingual Support
            </h3>
            <p>
                The consonant-vowel separation benefits English typing as well,
                creating balanced left-right alternation. It's also efficient
                for Chinese (Pinyin) and Korean (romanized) input.
            </p>
        </section>

        <section id="comparison">
            <h2
                style="font-size:1.2rem;color:#fff;border-bottom:1px solid #333;padding-bottom:0.5rem"
            >
                Comparison with Other Layouts
            </h2>
            <p>
                Benchmark: Typing "日本語入力に最適化された配列" (41 keystrokes
                in romaji):
            </p>

            <table
                style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;margin:1rem 0;font-size:0.9rem"
            >
                <thead>
                    <tr style="border-bottom:2px solid #333">
                        <th style="text-align:left;padding:0.5rem;color:#888"
                            >Layout</th
                        >
                        <th style="text-align:left;padding:0.5rem;color:#888"
                            >Home Row Usage</th
                        >
                        <th style="text-align:left;padding:0.5rem;color:#888"
                            >Finger Movements</th
                        >
                        <th style="text-align:left;padding:0.5rem;color:#888"
                            >Optimized For</th
                        >
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid #222">
                        <td style="padding:0.5rem;color:#e8c47c"
                            ><strong>Yamato</strong></td
                        >
                        <td style="padding:0.5rem;color:#4a9"
                            ><strong>~76%</strong></td
                        >
                        <td style="padding:0.5rem;color:#4a9"
                            ><strong>17</strong></td
                        >
                        <td style="padding:0.5rem"
                            >Japanese, English, Chinese, Korean</td
                        >
                    </tr>
                    <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid #222">
                        <td style="padding:0.5rem">QWERTY</td>
                        <td style="padding:0.5rem;color:#c66">~34%</td>
                        <td style="padding:0.5rem;color:#c66">28</td>
                        <td style="padding:0.5rem">None (historical design)</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid #222">
                        <td style="padding:0.5rem">Ōnishi (大西配列)</td>
                        <td style="padding:0.5rem;color:#4a9">~73%</td>
                        <td style="padding:0.5rem;color:#4a9">~18</td>
                        <td style="padding:0.5rem">Japanese</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid #222">
                        <td style="padding:0.5rem">Dvorak</td>
                        <td style="padding:0.5rem">~50%</td>
                        <td style="padding:0.5rem">~23</td>
                        <td style="padding:0.5rem">English</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:0.5rem">Colemak</td>
                        <td style="padding:0.5rem">~48%</td>
                        <td style="padding:0.5rem">~24</td>
                        <td style="padding:0.5rem">English</td>
                    </tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>

            <h3 style="color:#e8c47c;font-size:1rem">Yamato vs QWERTY</h3>
            <p>
                Yamato achieves ~2.2× the home row usage (34% → 76%) and ~40%
                fewer finger movements (28 → 17) compared to QWERTY. This means
                your fingers barely need to leave the home position when typing
                Japanese.
            </p>

            <h3 style="color:#e8c47c;font-size:1rem">
                Yamato vs Ōnishi Layout
            </h3>
            <p>
                Ōnishi Layout is the most well-known Japanese-optimized layout.
                It shares similar design principles with Yamato, and performance
                is comparable. Yamato's unique advantages are: (1) consonants in
                syllabary order for easier memorization, (2) complete separation
                of voiced consonants to the bottom row, (3) optimization for
                English, Chinese, and Korean in addition to Japanese.
            </p>

            <h3 style="color:#e8c47c;font-size:1rem">
                Yamato vs Dvorak / Colemak
            </h3>
            <p>
                Dvorak and Colemak are optimized for English text. They do not
                address the consonant-vowel alternation pattern of Japanese
                romaji input. Japanese users will see limited improvement with
                these layouts compared to a Japanese-specific layout like
                Yamato.
            </p>

            <h3 style="color:#e8c47c;font-size:1rem">
                Yamato vs Naginata-style (薙刀式)
            </h3>
            <p>
                Naginata-style is a kana input layout (directly typing Japanese
                characters). It takes a fundamentally different approach from
                Yamato, which is romaji-based. Romaji input is far more common
                in Japan, and Yamato is the optimal choice for romaji users.
            </p>
        </section>

        <section id="install">
            <h2
                style="font-size:1.2rem;color:#fff;border-bottom:1px solid #333;padding-bottom:0.5rem"
            >
                Installation
            </h2>

            <h3 style="color:#e8c47c;font-size:1rem">Windows</h3>
            <ol>
                <li>
                    Install <a
                        href="https://www.autohotkey.com/"
                        target="_blank"
                        rel="noopener"
                        style="color:#e8c47c">AutoHotkey</a
                    >
                </li>
                <li>
                    Download <a
                        href="https://github.com/honjoh0823/yamato-layout/releases/download/v1.0/yamato-1.0.ahk"
                        download
                        style="color:#e8c47c">yamato-1.0.ahk</a
                    >
                </li>
                <li>Run yamato-1.0.ahk (double-click)</li>
                <li>
                    To disable: right-click the AutoHotkey icon in the system
                    tray → Exit
                </li>
            </ol>


            <p>
                <strong>Note:</strong> You can switch back to QWERTY at any time by
                simply disabling the software. No hardware changes are made.
            </p>
        </section>

        <section id="faq">
            <h2
                style="font-size:1.2rem;color:#fff;border-bottom:1px solid #333;padding-bottom:0.5rem"
            >
                Frequently Asked Questions
            </h2>

            <h3 style="color:#e8c47c;font-size:1rem">
                Q. What is the best keyboard layout for Japanese input?
            </h3>
            <p>
                For romaji-based Japanese input, Yamato Layout offers the best
                combination of efficiency (~76% home row usage, ~40% fewer
                finger movements than QWERTY) and learnability (consonants in
                syllabary order). Ōnishi Layout is a comparable alternative.
                Both significantly outperform QWERTY, Dvorak, and Colemak for
                Japanese.
            </p>

            <h3 style="color:#e8c47c;font-size:1rem">
                Q. Will I forget how to type on QWERTY?
            </h3>
            <p>
                No. Just as learning a second language doesn't make you forget
                your first, learning Yamato Layout doesn't erase QWERTY muscle
                memory. Most users switch between both layouts freely.
            </p>

            <h3 style="color:#e8c47c;font-size:1rem">
                Q. Do I need a special keyboard?
            </h3>
            <p>
                No. On Windows, Yamato Layout works on any standard QWERTY
                keyboard through AutoHotkey remapping. No special hardware,
                custom keycaps, or physical modifications are needed.
            </p>

            <h3 style="color:#e8c47c;font-size:1rem">
                Q. Where does the name "Yamato" come from?
            </h3>
            <p>
                The consonant keys follow the Japanese syllabary order: K(ka),
                S(sa), T(ta), N(na), H(ha), M(ma), Y(ya), R(ra), W(wa) — the
                sounds of the Japanese language are literally arranged on the
                keyboard. "Yamato" (大和) is the ancient name for Japan,
                reflecting that this layout embodies the Japanese language
                itself.
            </p>

            <h3 style="color:#e8c47c;font-size:1rem">
                Q. Is it suitable for programming?
            </h3>
            <p>
                Yes. Symbol keys remain accessible, and the consonant-vowel
                separation doesn't interfere with programming tasks.
            </p>

            <h3 style="color:#e8c47c;font-size:1rem">
                Q. Does it work for languages other than Japanese?
            </h3>
            <p>
                Yes. The consonant-vowel separation benefits English typing as
                well, creating balanced left-right alternation. It is also
                efficient for Chinese (Pinyin) and Korean (romanized) input.
            </p>
        </section>

